# Untitled

All-Polymer Solar Cells Based on a Conjugated Polymer Containing Siloxane-Functionalized Side Chains with Efficiency over 10%
A novel wide-bandgap conjugated copolymer based on an inside-functionalized benzotriazole building block containing a siloxane-terminated side-chain is developed. This copolymer is successfully used to fabricate highly efficient all-polymer solar cells (all-PSCs) processed at room temperature with the green-solvent 2-methyl-tetrahydrofuran. When paired with a naphthalene diimide-based polymer electron-acceptor, the all-PSC exhibits a maximum power conversion efficiency (PCE) of \(10.1\%\), which is the highest value so far reported for an all-PSC. Of particular interest is that the PCE remains \(9.4\%\) after thermal annealing at \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\). The resulting high efficiency is attributed to a combination of high and balanced bulky charge-carrier mobility, favorable face-on orientation, and high crystallinity. These observations indicate that the resulting copolymer can be a promising candidate toward high-performance all-PSCs for practical applications.
Organic solar cells (OSCs) are lightweight, mechanically flexible, and can be processed using fast roll-to-roll printing techniques.[1] Recently, a specific type of OSCs, all-polymer solar cells (all-PSCs), have attracted significant interest.[2] The active layers of all-PSCs consist of a p-type polymer and an n-type polymer. This combination results in high photochemical, thermal, and mechanical stability, and is suitable for large-area production.[3] Although improved power conversion efficiencies (PCEs) have been achieved for single-junction devices,[4] the overall performance of all-PSC devices still lags behind those of conventional fullerene and nonfullerene OSCs.[5]
To improve the photovoltaic performance of all-PSCs, novel n-type polymers have been developed based on naphthalene
diimide or perylene diimide moieties. These polymers have enabled all-PSCs with PCEs \(>8\%\) to be fabricated.[6] Alternatively, the PCEs may be improved by the development of novel p-type polymeric materials suitable for pairing with typical n-type copolymers, such as poly{[N,N'-bis(2-octyldodecyl)-naphthalene-1,4,5,8-bis(dicarboximide)-2,6-diyl]-alt-5,5'-(2,2'-bithiophene)} (with the commercial name of N2200).[7] N2200 has dual absorption characteristics, with a high-energy band at about \(400\,\mathrm{nm}\) associated with a low-energy band at \(600{-}850\,\mathrm{nm}\). Hence, it is highly desirable to develop copolymers with medium or wide bandgaps that have suitable energy level alignments and complementary absorption with N2200.[8]
For instance, Li and co-workers developed a copolymer containing a difluorobenzotriazole unit that exhibited complementary absorption and good miscibility with the n-type copolymer N2200, resulting in a high PCE of \(8.27\%\).[6a] Recently, we demonstrated all-PSCs based on a donor–acceptor type conjugated polymer PTzBI with PCEs \(>9\%\).[6b] PTzBI is composed of a pyrrolo[3,4-f]benzotriazole-5,7-dione (TzBI) building block as the electron-deficient unit and benzodithiophene as the electron-rich unit. This copolymer exhibits a relatively wide bandgap of \(1.8\,\mathrm{eV}\) and maximum absorption at \(590\,\mathrm{nm}\).[9] Hence, it can be paired with N2200 to achieve improved light absorbance. Moreover, PTzBI:N2200 blend films can be processed using an environmentally friendly solvent 2-methyl-tetrafuran (MeTHF) to produce optimal film morphology for exciton diffusion and charge carrier transportation.[6b]
For conjugated polymers, side chains play a critical role in solubility, processability, intermolecular packing, and the morphology of bulk-heterojunction films.[10] In the present study, we attempted to improve the photovoltaic performance of all-PSCs based on PTzBI:N2200 by incorporating an alkyl side chain terminated with a 1,1,1,3,5,5,5-heptamethyltrisiloxane unit into the triazole moiety of the TzBI unit. This siloxane-functionalized substituent increased the solubility of the resulting copolymer poly{[4,8-bis(5-(2-ethylhexyl)thiophen-2-yl)benzo[1,2-b:4,5-b']dithiophene-co-4,8-di(thien-2-yl)-2-(6-(1,1,1,3,5,5,5-heptamethyltrisiloxan-3-yl)hexyl)-6-octyl[1,2,3]triazolo[4,5-f]isoindole-5,7(2H,6H)-dione} (PTzBI-Si, Scheme 1) in MeTHF.
Besides, it facilitates the formation of optimal film morphology, and the preferential face-on orientation of polymer chains, resulting in well-balanced charge carrier mobility of blend films. The resulting all-PSC demonstrated an unprecedented PCE of \(10.1\%\) with a high fill factor (FF) of \(73.39\%\pm0.59\%\) and a high short-circuit current \((J_{\mathrm{SC}})\) of \(15.57\pm0.17\ \mathrm{mA\ cm^{-2}}\). This PCE is the highest yet reported for single-junction all-PSCs.
PTzBI-Si was synthesized via the alkylation of 6-octyl-4,8-di(thiophen-2-yl)-[1,2,3]triazolo[4,5-f]isoindole-5,7(1H,6H)-dione (1) using 6-bromohex-1-ene, which yielded the intermediate compound TzBI-C6 (2). By treating (2) with 1,1,1,3,5,5,5-heptamethyltrisiloxane in the presence of Karstedt catalyst, the siloxane-functionalized intermediate TzBI-Si (3) was obtained. Compound 3 was brominated using \(N\)-bromosuccinimide to give the target monomer \(\mathrm{Pr}_{2}\)-TzBI-Si (4) in a good yield of \(87.5\%\). The target copolymer PTzBI-Si was obtained in \(85.6\%\) yield through Stille polymerization of (4) with the distannylated compound 4,8-bis(5-(2-ethylhexyl)thiophen-2-yl)benzo[1,2-b:4,5-b']dithiophene-2,6-diyl)bis-trimethylstannane (5). The number-average molecular weight \(\left(M_{\mathrm{n}}\right)\) and polydispersity index of PTzBI-Si were estimated to be \(38.4\ \mathrm{kg\ mol^{-1}}\) and 2.45, respectively, as evaluated by gel permeation chromatography with a linear polystyrene standard and using tetrahydrofuran as the eluent (Table S1, Supporting Information). From the differential scanning calorimetry measurements (Figure S1, Supporting Information), no discernible glass transition peak was observed up to \(400\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in the second heating and cooling cycle while a weak melting peak could be seen in the second heating process, indicating its crystalline property.
The solubility of PTzBI-Si in MeTHF as well as PTzBI and N2200 was evaluated by diluting the concentrated solution.
PTzBI-Si exhibited a high limit of solubility of about \(50\ \mathrm{mg\ mL^{-1}}\) in MeTHF at room temperature \((25\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\), which is higher than the previous polymer PTzBI with a limit of solubility of about \(35\ \mathrm{mg\ mL^{-1}}\) (see Figure S2 and Table S2, Supporting Information). Because N2200 used here has a high number average molecular weight \(\left(M_{\mathrm{n}}=75.1\ \mathrm{kg\ mol^{-1}}\right.\), polydispersity index of 1.78, see Table S1, Supporting Information), we dissolve N2200 in MeTHF with concentration of \(10\ \mathrm{mg\ mL^{-1}}\) at a raised temperature of \(110\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to achieve uniform solution within \(30\ \mathrm{min}\). The solution becomes very viscous but no precipitation emerged after cooling down to \(25\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), which indicates that the solubility of N2200 in MeTHF is limited to \(10\ \mathrm{mg\ mL^{-1}}\).
PTzBI-Si exhibited peak absorption at about \(586\ \mathrm{nm}\) and an absorption onset of about \(695\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Figure 1a), corresponding to an optical bandgap of \(1.78\ \mathrm{eV}\). Its absorption profile complements that of N2200 film, which has two distinct absorption bands located at 300–440 and \(500{-}860\ \mathrm{nm}\). The broader absorption \((300{-}860\ \mathrm{nm})\) exhibited by the PTzBI-Si:N2200 (1:0.5, wt:wt) blend film indicates that the addition of PTzBI-Si can enhance the light-harvesting ability of N2200-based devices. The frontier orbital levels of PTzBI-Si were estimated by electrochemical cyclic voltammetry (Figure S3, Supporting Information). The highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) and lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) energy levels were calculated to be \(-5.31\) and \(-3.10\ \mathrm{eV}\), respectively, based on the onsets of oxidation and reduction potential. The energy levels of PTzBI-Si can be well-matched with N2200 that has an HOMO/LUMO of \(-5.81/-3.84\ \mathrm{eV}\), respectively (Figure S3, Supporting Information). The energy level alignments of the relevant components of the all-PSC devices discussed herein are illustrated in Figure 1b.
To characterize the photovoltaic properties of devices based on PTzBI-Si:N2200, we fabricated all-PSCs with a conventional indium tin oxide (ITO)/poly(3,4-ethylenedioxythiophene):polystyrene sulfonate (PEDOT:PSS, P VP Al 4083)/active layer/ PFNDI-Br \((\approx\!5\ \mathrm{nm})/\mathrm{Ag}\) structure. A thin layer of poly[(9,9-bis(3′- \((N,N\)-dimethyl)-N-ethylammonium)propyl)-2,7-fluorene)- \(^{.}N,5^{\prime}\) - bis( \(^{2,2^{\prime}}\) -thiophene)-2,6-naphthalene-1,4,5,8-tetracaboxylic- \(N,N^{\prime}\) - di(2-ethylhexyl)imide)dibromide (PFNDI-Br) was used as the cathode interlayer to facilitate charge extraction.[11] MeTHF that has a purity \(\geq99.5\%\) and contains \(150{-}400\;\mathrm{ppm}\) butylated hydroxytoluene as stabilizer was used as the processing solvent without further purification. It is noteworthy that MeTHF is cleaner than the halogen solvents such as chloroform and chlorobenzene according to the Chemwatch hazard rating and the Global Harmonized System of common solvents (see Table S3, Supporting Information).[12] The current density–voltage \((J\!\!-\!\!V)\) characteristics of these devices were measured under simulated solar illumination (AM 1.5G, \(100\,\mathrm{\mW\cm}^{-2}\) ); the corresponding curves and parameters are shown in Figure 2a and Table 1, respectively.
The initial optimization of the PTzBI-Si-based all-PSCs was carried out with an blend ratio of 1:0.5 (wt:wt), which is according to the previously developed PTzBI-based device.[6b] Devices based on PTzBI-Si:N2200 (1:0.5, wt:wt) blend film exhibited a high PCE of \(8.3\%\) , a \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of \(0.873\;\mathrm{~V},\) a \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) of \(13.76\,\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}\) , and an FF of \(68.95\%\) . Because MeTHF was used as the processing solvent, solvent additives (SAs) were not required for device fabrication; most commonly used SAs are halogenated and/or aromatic compounds, which are toxic
The external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra show that both devices exhibited broad responses from 300 to about \(860\,\mathrm{nm}\) (Figure 2b). The annealed device exhibited a significantly higher response across almost the entire spectral region than the as-cast device. \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) values were calculated by integrating the relevant current densities from the EQE spectra and determined to be 13.12 and \(14.84~\mathrm{mA~cm}^{-2}\) for the as-cast and annealed devices, respectively. These values correspond well with the measured \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\).
To characterize the photoinduced carrier generation and charge collection behavior of the annealed device, we measured the photocurrent density \((J_{\mathrm{ph}})\) versus internal voltage \((V_{\mathrm{eff}})\) under illumination at \(100\;\mathrm{mW}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\). \(J_{\mathrm{ph}}=J_{\mathrm{L}}-J_{\mathrm{D}}\), where \(J_{\mathrm{L}}\) and \(J_{\mathrm{D}}\) represent current densities measured under illumination
and in the dark, respectively. \(V_{\mathrm{eff}}\,=\,V_{\mathrm{bi}}\,-\,V_{\mathrm{appl}}\) , where \(V_{\mathrm{bi}}\) is the voltage when \(J_{\mathrm{ph}}=0\) (the built-in voltage), and \(V_{\mathrm{appl}}\) is the applied voltage.[14] As shown in Figure 3a, \(J_{\mathrm{ph}}\) saturates at high \(V_{\mathrm{eff}}\) values of about \(2\ \mathrm{V}.\) At the saturation point, the internal field is large enough to sweep out all of the generated carriers to the electrodes. Thus, the saturated photocurrent density \((J_{\mathrm{sat}})\) is determined mainly by the number of generated carriers, instead of the charge collection ability. Therefore, the maximum photoinduced carrier generation rate \(G_{\mathrm{max}}\) can be calculated using \(G_{\mathrm{max}}=J_{\mathrm{sat}}/(q\!\cdot\!L)\) , where \(q\) is the elementary charge and \(L\) is the active layer thickness. The \(J_{\mathrm{sat}}\) of the annealed device was found to be \(16.6\ \mathrm{mA\cm^{-2}}\) ; thus, \(G_{\mathrm{max}}=9.43\,\times\,10^{27}\,\,\mathrm{m}^{-3}\,\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) (Table S8, Supporting Information). This high \(G_{\mathrm{max}}\) value indicates that the PTzBI-Si-based all-PSC device exhibited good exciton dissociation and carrier generation ability. This result was confirmed through analysis of the photoluminescence (PL) spectra of the pristine polymers and polymer blends (discussed in the following section). The charge collection capacity was evaluated by determining the charge collection probability \((P_{\mathrm{C}})\) with respect to \(V_{\mathrm{eff}}\) in the low voltage region (inset of Figure 3a). \(P_{\mathrm{C}}\) was obtained by normalizing \(J_{\mathrm{ph}}\) with \(J_{\mathrm{sat}}\) \((P_{\mathrm{C}}=J_{\mathrm{ph}}/J_{\mathrm{sat}})\) .[15] The annealed device exhibited a high \(P_{\mathrm{C}}\) of \(90.6\%\) under shortcircuit conditions, and a good \(P_{\mathrm{C}}\) of \(73.6\%\) at the maximum power point, indicating that efficient charge collection occurred in the device.
The exciton dissociation and charge transfer behavior in the polymer blend was also evaluated by PL measurements (Figure 3c,d; Figure S8a, Supporting Information). We excited
PTzBI-Si and N2200 at wavelengths close to their absorption peaks. Hence, \(550\;\mathrm{nm}\) radiation was used to excite PTzBI-Si and 380 and \(710\;\mathrm{nm}\) radiation were used to excite N2200. Figure 3c shows that the PL emission of PTzBI-Si was centered at \(709\;\mathrm{nm}\) when excited at \(550\;\mathrm{nm}\); this was largely quenched by N2200 in the PTzBI-Si:N2200 (1:0.5, wt:wt) blend film. The PL emission peak of N2200 was \(832\;\mathrm{nm}\) after excitation at either 710 or \(380\;\mathrm{nm}\), and was almost completely quenched in the polymer blend film (Figure 3d; Figure S8a, Supporting Information). These results indicate that efficient charge transfer occurred in the device, and that the hole transfer from N2200 to PTzBI-Si was more efficient than the electron transfer from PTzBI-Si to N2200.
To further characterize the charge recombination properties of the annealed device, we measured its \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) as a function of light intensity from 5 to \(100\;\mathrm{mW\;cm}^{-2}\) (Figure 3b). The power law dependence of \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) upon illumination intensity can be expressed as \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\propto(P_{\mathrm{light}})^{5}\), where \(P_{\mathrm{light}}\) is the illumination intensity and \(S\) is the exponential factor.[16] It has been reported that \(S\) is close to unity when the bimolecular recombination in an organic solar cell is weak.[17] The \(S\) value extracted from the slope of the fitted line in the \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}-P_{\mathrm{light}}\) plot for the annealed device based on PTzBI-Si:N2200 (1:0.5, wt:wt) was 0.98. This value is very close to unity, implying that the bimolecular recombination in this device was weak. This result is consistent with the relatively high FF value of \(73.76\%\).
To investigate the effect of siloxane-terminated side-chains on charge transport ability, we evaluated the charge carrier mobility of the blend film using the space-charge limited current method. The hole and electron mobilities were measured using hole- and electron-only devices with ITO/PEDOT:PSS/active layer \(\mathrm{MoO}_{3}/\mathrm{Ag}\) and ITO/ZnO/active layer/PFNDI-Br/Ag architectures, respectively. From the \(J^{1/2}-\mathrm{V}\) characteristics shown in Figure S8b,c (Supporting Information), the annealed device based on PTzBI-Si:N2200 (1:0.5, wt:wt) exhibited high hole and electron mobilities of \(1.87\;\times\;10^{-3}\) (average of hole-only devices with different active layer thickness) and \(2.88\times10^{-4}\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\;\mathrm{V}^{-1}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\), respectively; these results are favorable for devices with high \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) values. When compared with the typical highly efficient polymers poly{4,8-bis(4,5-didecylthiophen-2-yl)-benzo[1,2-b:4,5-b']dithiophene-co-5,10-bis(4-hexylthiophen-2-yl)-naphtho[1,2-c:5,6-c']bis([1,2,5]thiadiazole)} (PBDT-DTNT) based on naphtho[1,2-c:5,6-c']bis[1,2,5]thiadiazole (NT) unit and PTB7-Th based on thieno[3,4-b]thiophene unit, the hole mobility of PTzBI-Si:N2200 is much higher than PBDT-DTNT:N2200 \((2.5\times10^{-4}\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\;\mathrm{V}^{-1}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1})\) and PTB7-Th:N2200 \((4.9\times10^{-4}\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\;\mathrm{V}^{-1}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1})\) blend films, and is even comparable with the hole mobility of BHJ films consisting of these polymers and fullerene derivatives (see Table S9, Supporting Information).[18] Note the high hole mobility in the vertical direction between two electrodes is favorable for charge carrier transport in the resulting all-PSCs, which is consistent with the high \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) of the resulting devices.[19] In addition, these balanced charge mobilities of PTzBI-Si:N2200 reduced bimolecular recombination, and thereby increased the FF value of the device.[20]
To further characterize the charge carrier mobility of this copolymer, we also fabricated bottom-gate, top-contact organic field effect transistors, which contain highly n-doped silicon and silver film \((60\;\mathrm{nm})\) as the back gate and the source/drain
To further characterize the charge carrier mobility of this copolymer, we also fabricated bottom-gate, top-contact organic field effect transistors, which contain highly n-doped silicon and silver film \((60\;\mathrm{nm})\) as the back gate and the source/drain electrodes, respectively. The PTzBI and PTzBI-Si neat films \((\approx40\;\mathrm{nm})\) were spin-coated on an octyltrichlorosilane-treated \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) gate insulator and then annealed in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) glovebox at different temperatures. From the slope of \((I_{\mathrm{DS}})^{1/2}-\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{G}}\) curves \((I_{\mathrm{DS}}\) is the drain–source current and \(\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{G}}\) is the gate voltage), the hole mobilities of PTzBI- and PTzBI-Si-based as-cast field effect transistors are calculated as \(3.17\times10^{-3}\) and \(2.67\times10^{-3}\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\;\mathrm{V}^{-1}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\), respectively (Figure S9 and Table S10, Supporting Information). Annealing at \(130\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) acquires higher mobility of \(9.68\times10^{-3}\) and \(2.34\times10^{-2}\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\;\mathrm{V}^{-1}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\), while higher temperature of \(200\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) further promotes the mobility of PTzBI and PTzBI-Si to be \(3.13\times10^{-2}\) and \(5.13\times10^{-2}\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\;\mathrm{V}^{-1}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\), respectively. It is clear that PTzBI-Si exhibits improved mobility than the previous polymer PTzBI under different annealing conditions.[10a]
Grazing-incidence wide-angle X-ray scattering (GIWAXS) was carried out to determine the correlation between molecular microstructure and photovoltaic performance. Figure 4 shows the 2D GIWAXS patterns, and relevant line-cuts profiles, for PTzBI-Si, N2200, as-cast, and annealed PTzBI-Si:N2200 films. From the pattern of PTzBI-Si neat film shown in Figure 4a, a distinct (010) \(\pi-\pi\) stacking peak \((q=1.64\;\mathrm{\AA}^{-1})\) is observed out-of-plane (OOP) and a corresponding lamellar scattering (100) is located in-plane (IP). Such a mirrored reflection between (100) and (010) indicates a dominating face-on orientation relative to the substrate. The neat N2200 film exhibits a well-defined OOP (010) peak \((q=1.50\;\mathrm{\AA}^{-1})\) with no defused ring and a corresponding intense IP (100) peak (Figure 4b), implying the exclusive face-on orientation of N2200 backbone. In addition, the feature IP (001) peak \((q=0.45\;\mathrm{\AA}^{-1})\) of N2200 backbone can be evidently observed in Figure 4b,e, which indicates the excellent crystallinity of N2200 when processed with MeTHF.[5]
For the blend films, both PTzBI-Si and N2200 maintain their own packing orientations (Figure 4c,d). Consider that the preferential face-on orientation of both PTzBI-Si and N2200, it is reasonable to surmise that these two polymers will adopt face-to-face stacking at the boundary between the donor and the acceptor crystalline area.[21] Since strong \(\pi\)-orbital overlap of polymer donor and polymer acceptor is crucial for efficient free charge generation,[22] the face-to-face stacking between PTzBI-Si and N2200 will contribute largely to the charge transfer in all-PSCs, which is in accordance with the PL quenching results. Besides, the annealed blend film displays stronger scattering signals with much higher OOP (010), IP (100), and IP (001) peaks (Figure 4d) than those observed from the as-cast film (Figure 4c), indicating that thermal annealing leads to significantly improved crystallinity of both components. The predominant face-on orientation and high crystallinity of the annealed film is desirable for improved charge transport in all-PSCs (Figure S8b,c, Supporting Information), which results in high photovoltaic performance.
The phase separation morphology of the PTzBI-Si:N2200 films was characterized by tapping-mode atomic force microscopy (AFM) (Figure S10, Supporting Information) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) (Figure S11, Supporting Information). The as-cast and annealed blend films exhibited very similar root mean square values (obtained from AFM) and almost identical phase separation profiles (determined by TEM). These results indicate that thermal annealing had little impact on the aggregation behavior or phase separation
morphology of the blend films. The stable film morphology under long-term heating at \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) is consistent with the stable photovoltaic performance of the resulting devices. However, the slightly decreased PCE (about \(3\%\) decline regarding to the original value) after \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) annealing for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) might be ascribed to that the extension of thermal annealing (TA) time slightly undermine the crystallinity and phase purity of the active layer, leading to increased charge recombination in the device and thus slightly decreased FF and PCE.[23]
In summary, our results demonstrate the successful use of PTzBI-Si into all-PSCs with unprecedented PCEs of greater than \(10\%\). The incorporation of TzBI resulted in a copolymer with a low-lying HOMO and an absorption spectrum complementary to that of N2200. Moreover, the grafted siloxane-functionalized side chains resulted in excellent solubility in the green-solvent MeTHF and improved the charge carrier mobility of the resulting blend films. This molecular design strategy can be used to develop highly efficient photovoltaic materials that can be processed using environmentally friendly solvents.
